# The verbatim archive stores user text byte-for-byte, including trailing spaces
# and tabs the user actually typed. `git diff --check` must not "fix" them, or
# the archive would stop being byte-for-byte — the one property it exists for.
# `-whitespace` disables all whitespace rules for these paths; nothing else about
# the files changes.
docs/verbatim.md        -whitespace
docs/verbatim/*.md      -whitespace

# After the D-036 move the archive lives here. The protection has to follow the
# files: `git diff --check` must not "fix" a trailing space the user actually
# typed, and it only respects that per path, so naming the old location alone
# would leave the archive unprotected at its new one.
.brain/verbatim.md      -whitespace
.brain/verbatim/*.md    -whitespace

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# --- THE MOVE: docs/ -> .brain/ (the operator's ruling, D-036) ---------------
# The agent's own documentation used to live under docs/; it now lives under
# .brain/. Both the destination AND the old names are marked, for two different
# reasons, and both are needed:
#
#   .brain/**        is where those files are now.
#   docs/<old names> are named because the public branch's strip commit deletes
#                    them. If one is ever restored from an old checkout, or a
#                    partial merge re-adds it, these attributes stop the merge
#                    from resurrecting it on the private side. A strip-set entry
#                    for a path that does not exist costs nothing and catches
#                    the regression that would otherwise publish it.
# PRIVATE-ONLY PATHS — `merge=ours`
#
# WHY THIS BLOCK EXISTS. Decision D-035 splits this repository in two: the
# PRIVATE repo is the whole thing, the PUBLIC repo is a sanitized copy produced
# from the allowlist in bin/public-manifest.txt. The split direction is
# private -> public, and the public repository's default branch therefore
# carries a STRIP COMMIT that deletes every private-only path.
#
# The consequence, if nothing here stops it: merging the public branch BACK
# into this private repository propagates those deletions, and the private-only
# files are deleted from the private repo too — by a commit that was authored in
# a repository that was never supposed to know those files existed. That is the
# one way this split can destroy the thing it is protecting.
#
# `merge=ours` is git's BUILT-IN merge driver. On a path carrying it, when the
# other side deletes the file and this side modified it, the merge KEEPS THIS
# SIDE'S VERSION and never takes the deletion. It needs no `git config`, no
# `.gitattributes` in the other repository, and nothing set up on the runner —
# per-path scoping here is the whole mechanism, and it is the documented way to
# express "this file exists only on my side of the merge".
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. `merge=ours` resolves CONFLICTS; it does not
# stop a delete/modify pair from being auto-resolved quietly in this file's
# favour — which is the point. It is one-directional by design: it protects
# these paths from the public branch, and it says nothing about a change that
# travels private -> public. That direction is governed by the allowlist and by
# bin/check-public-surface.php, which fails the build if any path listed here is
# ever tracked in the public tree.
#
# `merge` and `-whitespace` are SEPARATE attribute namespaces, so the two blocks
# above and below compose on the same paths: `docs/verbatim.md` and
# `docs/verbatim/*.md` get BOTH the whitespace exemption above and the merge
# protection here. Nothing above was changed to make room for this.
#
# The list is decision D-035's private-only set. Keep it in step with
# bin/check-public-surface.php's PRIVATE_ONLY array — a private path present in
# one and absent from the other is a real defect, not a cosmetic drift.
.brain/**                     merge=ours
.ledger/**                    merge=ours
.opencode-ci/**               merge=ours
inspection-snapshot/**        merge=ours
docs/verbatim.md              merge=ours
docs/verbatim/**              merge=ours
docs/subagents/**             merge=ours
docs/evidence/**              merge=ours
docs/test-reports/**          merge=ours
docs/plans/**                 merge=ours
docs/decisions.md             merge=ours
docs/agent-registry.md        merge=ours
docs/agent-identities.md      merge=ours
HANDOFF.md                    merge=ours
.warm-ledger.json             merge=ours
.credential-guard-baseline    merge=ours

